     "start_time": "2026-07-29T00:07:33.538727+00:00",
     "status": "completed"
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "**Concept:** a stacking ensemble trains a meta-model on the **out-of-fold predictions** of several level-0 models, rather than on the raw features directly. The idea is that each level-0 model (LogReg, RF, XGBoost, LightGBM) makes different kinds of mistakes, and a meta-model can learn when to trust which one — this only helps if the level-0 models are meaningfully different from each other, not just differently-tuned versions of the same idea.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Walk-forward passthrough is the part that's easy to get wrong here:** the meta-model's training rows must be *out-of-fold* predictions from the level-0 models (predictions on data those models didn't see), computed walk-forward, in the same style as `cv_no_scaling()`/`cv_regression()` — not in-sample predictions, which would hand the meta-model a leaked, over-optimistic signal to learn from."
   ]
  },
